      "text": "Einkorn (diploid, 2n=14, genome AA)\n  Triticum monococcum var. aegilopoides — wild einkorn, Karacadağ Mts, Turkey\n  T. monococcum — domesticated einkorn, ~10,500 BP\n  Characteristics: hulled (grain enclosed in glume), small, low yield\n  Still grown in: Turkey, Italy (farro piccolo)\n    │\n    │ Natural hybridization with Aegilops speltoides (BB genome)\n    │ + chromosome doubling (allopolyploidy)\n    ▼\nEmmer (tetraploid, 2n=28, genome AABB)\n  T. turgidum ssp. dicoccoides — wild emmer\n  T. turgidum ssp. dicoccum — domesticated emmer, ~9,000 BP\n  Higher yield, wider range than einkorn\n  Bread made from emmer = most Egyptian bread\n  Modern descendants: durum wheat (pasta!), Kamut, farro medio\n    │\n    │ Natural hybridization with Aegilops tauschii (goatgrass, DD genome)\n    │ + chromosome doubling\n    ▼\nBread wheat (hexaploid, 2n=42, genome AABBDD)\n  T. aestivum — ~8,500 BP\n  What \"flour\" means in most of the world\n  Gluten quality from D genome — stretchy, traps CO₂ → light bread\n  ~95% of global wheat production\n```"
